===== shape coverage: the adapter, both wire forms =====
# M4: the adapter site. One facade type, two wire forms.
#   Checked by state and not by payload: P4.1's `error` is a sentence in all
#   200 elements, so the payload exercises the ONE case that works. The states
#   where the map is not injective never occur in it.

state              -> nested wire             nested round trip    -> plain wire              plain round trip
Invalid            <field absent>             ok                   ""                         ok
Ok                 success=true, error=""     ok                   ""                         LOSES -> Invalid
Error("boom")      success=false, error="boom" ok                   "boom"                     ok

#   The nested form round-trips every state. The PLAIN form cannot: `Ok` and
#   `Invalid` both encode to the empty string, so one of them must come back
#   wrong whatever the adapter author chooses. This one returns `Invalid`,
#   which loses `Ok`; the intuitive alternative returns `Ok`, which silently
#   claims success for a task that reported no outcome at all. That is the
#   defect design/SHAPES.md says only a byte corpus catches, and the corpus
#   as it stands does not: no payload reaches either state.

#   The nested site has a worse problem, reported and not fixed: the payload
#   generator fills TaskOutput.success and TaskOutput.error INDEPENDENTLY, so
#   P2.x contains `success=true, error=<sentence>` -- a state no adapter over
#   {Ok, Error(d)} can represent at all. Counted over 200 elements the only
#   combinations present are (true, non-empty) and (false, non-empty); the
#   success state (true, empty) never occurs.

VERDICT: every shape above is exercised and every arm agrees.

